WHY NOT master
--------------
master (67bf1e0a, 2022-05-15) is newer and its `+description` already speaks
enumitem 3, which would have saved one of the two changes below. It is
unusable here anyway: master's document added a "Unicode を用いた数式の記述"
section that writes `${ x ÷ y }`, and a raw Unicode character in MATH mode is a
SATySFi 0.0.7 feature — 0.0.6's lexer (`lexer.mll:128`, `mathsymboltop`) accepts
only `+-*/:=<>~.,\`?`. This port targets 0.0.6 and rejects it, correctly.
Upstream SATySFi 0.0.11 does accept it and then recurses forever on master's
document (>12 CPU-minutes, 1.8 GB, killed), which is consistent with master's
own opam pinning `satysfi {>= "0.0.6" & < "0.0.7"}`.

TWO CHANGES TO THE VENDORED SOURCE
----------------------------------
Both are recorded at the site as well; this is the index.

1. `doc/azmath.saty` — enumitem 2 -> 3. Lifted verbatim from azmath's own
   master branch (commit e6775b6, "Bump enumitem to 3"): the `+dd` helper and
   the `+description<...>` rewrite. enumitem 2 gave `+description` an `itemize`
   argument, enumitem 3 gives it a `block-text` of `+ditem`s, and
   `fidelity.py`'s `assemble_lib_root` merges every document's stage map into
   ONE lib root, so the corpus can hold exactly one enumitem — which is 3.
   Without this the document does not typecheck in EITHER engine.

2. `src/equation.satyh` — `group-by-`'s two `- 1`s dropped. Same index-vs-count
   `List.take`/`List.split-at` hazard `easytable/src/matrix.satyg`'s
   `Matrix.set-nth` already carries in this corpus. As written,
   `split-at (n - 1)` with `+gather`'s n = 1 splits at 0 against a COUNT-based
   `satysfi-base`, so the recursive `aux` is handed back the list it was given
   and never terminates. This is azmath's own bug, not either engine's:

     - a two-row `+gather` in a 13-line document overflows this port's 256 MB
       evaluator stack in 0.8 s AND upstream SATySFi 0.0.11's 8 MB one in 0.2 s;
     - it is not proportional to input size — one row terminates, two do not;
     - a ONE-row equation terminated but silently DROPPED its own contents
       (`take (1*1 - 1)` = `take 0` = `[]`), so `+eqn` rendered a blank line.

   With this fixed, upstream SATySFi 0.0.11 builds the whole document in 1.6 s
   (15 pages).

THE BLOCKER THAT IS NOW CLOSED
------------------------------
The port used to refuse this document outright:

  Error: register-destination can only be called during page breaking
         (from a page-break hook or a decoration)

`equation.satyh`'s `ib-annotation` anchors every `\label`ed equation by calling
`register-destination` from inside an `inline-graphics` callback, and
`primitives.rs`'s `prim_inline_graphics` documents its "eager-callback
shortcut": the callback runs at CONSTRUCTION time, where no page exists, rather
than at page-break time where upstream runs it. The gate itself is faithful —
upstream's `annotation.ml:15` raises `State.NotDuringPageBreak` outside the
window too, so relaxing it would have been the wrong fix.

It is fixed by RE-TIMING instead: the eager call records the request, the box
carries it as a `GraphicsElem::Destination` marker, and `fire_hooks` replays it
once the box has a page and a placed point. `layout-tests/probes/
inline_graphics_dest.saty` is the package-free confirmation (both engines
render it, y agreeing to 0.001pt) and
`crates/rustyfi-lang/tests/inline_graphics_dest.rs` is the regression test.

The document now builds unstubbed, and its anchors are REAL: 16 named
destinations, exactly upstream's 16, in the same right-hand tag column. Their
absolute positions carry the document's ordinary layout drift, but their
relative spacing does not — consecutive equation anchors sit 20.255pt apart
here against upstream's 20.134pt.

WHY THIS IS STILL NOT IN fidelity.py's `DOCS`
---------------------------------------------
Measured UNSTUBBED, with every fix in place:

    char_match=0.9720  chars: port=11092 upstream=11001 (missing=1 extra=92)
    pages: port=15 upstream=15 (+0)   lines: port=582 upstream=583 (-1)
    width_p95=0.233pt

`width_p95` 0.233pt makes this the second most accurate document in the corpus
on glyph geometry, behind xpath's 0.230. It was 0.926 — the worst — when these
sources were vendored, and the document did not build at all.

What the two remaining numbers are, so neither gets re-investigated:

  * `missing=1` is a single character. The 49 that used to be here were
    table-of-contents leader dots, and they were never a port defect NOR a font
    pin problem: `fidelity.py --gen-refs` was building the reference with the
    SATySFi installation's OWN fonts while the port rendered with its bundled
    ones, so `width_p95` and the dot count were reading a Junicode version gap.
    `assemble_satysfi_lib_root` now stages the port's `dist/fonts` into the
    `-C` root, and the deficit went 49 -> 1 from that line alone.

  * `extra=92` is upstream's ToUnicode gap, not port output. Upstream DRAWS
    11093 glyphs and poppler extracts 11001; the 92 it drops are exactly the
    CIDs upstream's own ToUnicode CMap has no entry for, and they are all
    variant glyphs — `two.st` x72, `zero.st` x5, `three.st` x3, `u1D458.st` x3,
    `infinity.st` x3, `one.st` x2, plus `integral.v1`/`summation.v1`/
    `product.v1`. The port maps them, so poppler reads 92 more out of its PDF.
    Note `port extracted 11092 == upstream drawn 11093 - 1`. The same holds in
    latexcmds (11 unmapped <-> 11 extra), easytable and figbox, so `chars_extra`
    corpus-wide is largely this, not a port difference.

Registering it is a six-line `DOCS` entry plus a decision about the reference
PDF (see below), not a code change:

    Doc(
        "azmath",
        "azmath/doc/azmath.saty",
        "azmath/doc/azmath.pdf",
        "display math: aligned equations, matrices, auto-sized parens, accents",
        {"enumitem": "enumitem/src"},   # azmath arrives by relative @import:
    ),

WHAT THE 49 LEADER DOTS WERE (RESOLVED — THE HARNESS, NOT THE FONT PIN)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
This entry is kept because the first diagnosis was WRONG in a way worth not
repeating.

The symptom: 49 missing characters, all table-of-contents leader dots, every
line uniformly short by ~6.2%. `stdjabook.satyh`'s `make-dots-line` emits
`round (w /' wdot)` dots, where `wdot` is the natural width of `{.}` plus 1pt —
a direct readout of the Latin font — so the deficit correctly identified a
FONT-METRIC difference rather than a content loss or a port bug.

The first conclusion was that the pin was at fault: the port downloads Junicode
**1.002** (upem 2048, period 520 = 0.2539 em) while the flake's SATySFi 0.0.11
bundles **2.222** (upem 1000, period 234 = 0.234 em), an 8.5% wider dot cell,
so the port fit 745 dots against upstream's 794. That measurement was right.
The remedy inferred from it — repin the port to 2.222 and regenerate the
corpus's committed reference PDFs — was not.

The actual cause is one line of the HARNESS: `fidelity.py --gen-refs` built the
reference with the SATySFi installation's own fonts, because
`assemble_satysfi_lib_root` deliberately did not copy `lib-rustyfi`. So every
`--gen-refs` measurement in this corpus was reading a Junicode version gap on
top of the layout difference. Staging the port's `dist/fonts` into the `-C`
root — which upstream searches before its own (`main.ml:1063`) — took azmath
from `missing=49, width_p95=0.926pt` to `missing=1, width_p95=0.386pt`, with no
change to the pin, no new download, and no reference regenerated.

The lesson, since it cost two investigations: when a metric implicates an
ASSET, check that both sides of the comparison were actually given the same
asset before concluding the asset is wrong.

THE REFERENCE PDF
-----------------
Not vendored, deliberately. Upstream's own `doc/azmath.pdf` in the 0.0.3
tarball was built from the UNPATCHED document — enumitem 2's `+description` and
the runaway `group-by-` — so it is not a render of the source in this
directory and would silently anchor the comparison to the wrong text.

Produce the real one the way `fidelity.py --gen-refs` does, from the flake's
SATySFi 0.0.11 (`nix build .#satysfi --no-link --print-out-paths`), with a `-C`
root holding `satysfi-base/src` as `base` and `enumitem/src` as `enumitem`:

    satysfi doc/azmath.saty -o doc/azmath.pdf -C <root>

Run it from `doc/`, so the document's `@import: ../src/azmath` resolves.
